Richard Bos wrote on 08/04/2003 10:13 AM:
> Stanislav Traykov <stanislavNoeZpam@tortoises.org> wrote:
> 
>> Art Willis wrote on 07/31/2003 12:27 PM:
>> > The point to me is that the current version (MacNetHack 3.4.1)
>> > seems to have a biased RNG.
> 
>> Changing the seed won't change the algorithm. Unless you define RANDOM,
>> Nethack uses one of the linear congruential RNG functions provided by
>> your C library.
> 
> Erm, no. Some compiles, at least, of Nethack use rand() if RANDOM is not
> #defined, and rand(), despite silly rumours, is not required to be a
> piss-poor version of a linear congruential RNG. It really all depends on
> which C library you use, and whether its writers have bothered to
> include a decent rand() - something they are certainly allowed to do.

Indeed. I incorrectly assumed rand to be 32bit LCRNG as opposed to 48bit
*rand48. I guess this was/is(?) the case for many proprietary systems
which lead nethack to choose the *rand48 functions on UNIX systems.

Perhaps some research should be done whether current systems generally
supply a decent rand. If this is so, there would be no reason to fall
back to *rand48 on systems with UNIX defined?

